= Ontology — Simple =

'''Ontology''' has two important meanings.

In philosophy, ontology asks:

<blockquote>What kinds of things exist?</blockquote>

For example, philosophers can ask whether numbers, properties, events, or relations exist in the same way ordinary objects do.

In computing and information science, an ontology is a structured description of a subject area.

It can say:

* which kinds of things exist in the model;
* what they are called;
* which [[Relation/Simple|relations]] connect them;
* which [[Property/Simple|properties]] they can have;
* which rules or constraints apply.

An ontology is not the same as a [[Taxonomy/Simple|taxonomy]]. A taxonomy is usually mostly about organizing categories. An ontology can describe many different kinds of relationships and formal rules.

An ontology is also not the same as the language used to write it. [[Web Ontology Language/Simple|OWL]] is one ontology language.

